\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A workflow net with a silent transition. Circles are places, white boxes visible transitions, and the black box a silent transition ${"τ}$. The token in the source place $i$ is a truck before the gate. The silent transition skips the yard stop, so the net also accepts a visit without any stop.}}{10}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces How ProSiT discovers and runs a simulation model. The alignment replays every recorded case on the net, so that each decision, also at silent transitions, becomes a training example. One probabilistic decision tree is learned per component, and the simulator executes the net with these trees.}}{20}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces A real ProSiT tree: the arrival model learned from the CTB training partition (ProSiT 1.0.4, Section~\ref {sec:exp_baseline}). The tree predicts the gap until the next truck arrives, in open minutes of the arrival calendar, from the hour and weekday of the previous arrival. Each leaf holds a fitted distribution. The simulator follows the conditions from the top to one leaf and draws the gap from that leaf. The means are those of the values the simulator draws. Sunday is closed in the arrival calendar, so no gaps are drawn there.}}{22}{figure.caption.13}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Timestamps of a single-stop visit. Filled: recorded; open: not recorded. The model places a free lane at the start of the recorded stop; the data cannot show whether a truck sometimes reports ready before a lane is free.}}{29}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Flow of the experiments. Every decision uses the training log; the test log reports the results. White: experiment; grey: what it passes on.}}{36}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Trucks per day and daily median turnaround time over the whole period (days with at least 300 visits). Blue: training log; orange: test log. The dashed line marks the split, the grey band the Easter holidays (3 to 6 April), the green band the corridor of 25 to 35 minutes.}}{38}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Turnaround time per visit in the training and the test log, with the corridor of 25 to 35 minutes. Minutes above 35 per visit: the mean of $\max (\mathrm {TTT}-35, 0)$ over all visits.}}{39}{figure.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Control flow of the simulation model: HM 0.7 as discovered in ProM (22 places, 11 visible and 34 silent transitions, the silent ones drawn as black bars). Each silent transition stands for one dependency between two activities.}}{59}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Routing tree of the silent transition from Gate In to RMG receive: probability that it fires when it is enabled. $n$: training situations in which the transition was enabled, in total once per visit (59,139).}}{60}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Waiting-time tree of block T13: minutes from a free lane to the start of the stop (Section~\ref {sec:sim_adaptations}). All leaves on one scale.}}{61}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Execution-time tree of RMG receives. The first splits are on the number of other trucks at the block.}}{61}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Share of fitting visits and precision of the discovered nets on the training log (ProM 6.15). Left: all nets. Right: the nets with more than 91\,\% fitting visits and a precision above 0.7. Hollow markers: unsound nets, values for diagnosis only. The prefix tree accepts exactly the training sequences and serves as a reference.}}{69}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Which training sequences each sound candidate accepts. Top: share of the training visits per sequence (logarithmic scale), from the most to the least frequent sequence. Middle: number of stops of each sequence. Bottom: one row per net; a coloured cell means that the net accepts the complete sequence. The six most frequent sequences cover 88.5\,\% of the visits, so a net that accepts few sequences can still fit most visits.}}{70}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Median turnaround time per Gate-In hour over the test period, observed and simulated with the same trucks (final configuration, seed 42).}}{80}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Load profiles, observed and simulated, in the test period (solid) and the training period (dotted); medians, means over five seeds. With three lanes, the states ``4+ trucks at the block'' and ``3+ other trucks'' cannot occur in the simulation.}}{80}{figure.caption.49}%
